/* ============================================================
   Group 203 — this session (2026-09-12), person-requested feasibility
      follow-up: mergeFiles' copy+sort step (not the preceding per-file
      read/parse, which was already the same either way) turned out to be
      the actual cost behind the observed "merge takes a few seconds" for
      large files, so it now skips the chunked copy-with-yield and the
      O(n log n) sort entirely when all sources' time ranges are pairwise
      disjoint — a plain concat in chronological source order is already
      sorted in that case. Any overlap (including an exact boundary
      timestamp match) still uses the original copy+sort path unchanged.
      Also adds probeFileTimeRange(file, formatId): reads only a small
      head chunk and a growing tail chunk (via getCompiledFormat, so it
      respects custom log formats, not just the default) to learn a
      file's first/last timestamp without loading or parsing it in full —
      groundwork for a future folder-watch minimap, not wired into any UI
      yet. 203a: disjoint ranges take the quick/concat path and still come
      out chronologically ordered regardless of input order. 203b:
      overlapping ranges still produce a sorted result via the original
      path. 203c: an exact boundary-timestamp match between two sources is
      treated as overlap, not disjoint. 203d: probeFileTimeRange on a
      normal file, a single-entry file, an empty file (returns null), and
      a file whose last line is a timestamp-less continuation line (falls
      back to the last real header line).
   ============================================================ */

